  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-106626",
      "knowledge_point_id": "494f9793-de3b-5a0e-8ee9-38a8bad0f970",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Hyperbola Given its Parametric Equations",
      "problem": "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent Identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{3}} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{3} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${tan}^{2}⁡t+1={sec}^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+1 & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}=1",
              "option": "0",
              "source_option": "a"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1",
              "option": "1",
              "source_option": "b"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}=1",
              "option": "2",
              "source_option": "c"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1",
              "option": "3",
              "source_option": "d"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}=1",
              "option": "4",
              "source_option": "e"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "difficulty": "moderate",
