  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-106682",
      "knowledge_point_id": "0dfdda62-1838-540f-bf4d-960353fc3706",
      "knowledge_point": "Parametrizing Part of a Vertical Hyperbola",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/assets/ab11a3dde0aebbd4088c498e37da48909f9a309ca9d4ab29bfcfa788d53e5c5e.png)\n\nOne branch of a hyperbola (shown above) is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$",
      "worked_solution": "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a single branch, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/assets/f825ca9f9431ca38b14ed141639d5cf4189f0fde0526e943ddb956e112c3e4ab.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $[-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ],$ we get only the lower branch.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe only the lower branch are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cup (\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ].\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "t\\in [-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cup (\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]",
              "option": "0",
              "source_option": "a"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "t\\in [-\\pi ,0],\\,t\\ne -\\frac{\\pi }{2}",
              "option": "1",
              "source_option": "b"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "t\\in (-\\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})",
              "option": "2",
              "source_option": "c"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "t\\in [0,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}",
              "option": "3",
              "source_option": "d"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})",
              "option": "4",
              "source_option": "e"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "difficulty": "moderate",
